# Spherical maximal functions and fractal dimensions of dilation sets

Statement surface for Theorems 1.1 and 1.2 of J. Roos and A. Seeger,
"Spherical maximal functions and fractal dimensions of dilation sets",
American Journal of Mathematics 145 (2023), no. 4, 1077--1110,
https://doi.org/10.1353/ajm.2023.a902955, arXiv:2004.00984v4,
https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.00984v4.

For a set of radii $E \subseteq [1,2]$ the spherical maximal operator $M_E$ takes the supremum
of the spherical means $A_t f$ over $t \in E$. Its type set $\mathcal T_E$ is the set of points
$(1/p, 1/q)$ for which $M_E$ is bounded from $L^p(\mathbb R^d)$ to $L^q(\mathbb R^d)$.
Theorem 1.1 shows that $\mathcal T_E$ contains an explicit region $\mathcal R(\beta, \gamma)$
determined by the upper Minkowski dimension $\beta$ and the quasi-Assouad dimension $\gamma$ of
$E$. Theorem 1.2 characterizes exactly which closed convex sets arise as closures of type sets,
and shows that they determine $\beta$ and (the minimal admissible) $\gamma$.

The definitions below are faithful copies of `LeanSpherical/Definitions.lean`. Points to note:

* The type set is formulated with uniform a priori estimates on Schwartz functions, for exponents
  $1 \le p, q \le \infty$.
* Covering numbers use closed balls in base-two logarithmic coordinates on the positive radii.
  For $E \subseteq [1,2]$ this is bi-Lipschitz equivalent to the Euclidean metric, so the
  resulting dimensions are the usual ones.
* The quasi-Assouad dimension is the limit as $\theta \to 1^-$ of the *upper* Assouad spectrum,
  in which windows of every length between $r^\theta$ and $1$ are allowed.
* In Theorem 1.2 (i) the nonemptiness of $E$, implicit in the paper, is an explicit hypothesis,
  and $W$ ranges over all subsets of $\mathbb R^2$ rather than of $[0,1]^2$; the two
  formulations are equivalent since every closed type set lies in $[0,1]^2$.
-/

